  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="signals" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.1 · one job per signal</span>
        <h2>Use each signal for what it does best.</h2>
        <p>Permission history, performance troubleshooting, and long-lived operating facts need different guarantees and retention. Dekopon keeps them separate.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "Broker audit") }}</div><h3>What did the broker allow?</h3><p>An owner-only, hash-linked file keeps every decision and final result. A separate local checkpoint helps detect missing history.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "OpenTelemetry traces") }}</div><h3>What ran, and how long did it take?</h3><p>Spans follow model turns, scripts, commands, permission checks, provider work, and safe HTTP details with clear timing and parentage.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "OpenTelemetry logs") }}</div><h3>What should stay searchable?</h3><p>The runner sends costs, refusals, errors, and optional transcripts. The broker writes structured operating logs for an external collector.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Telemetry is not proof of permission.</strong>
        <p>A telemetry backend may sample data, delete old records, reject a batch, or go offline. The broker does not depend on it. The hash-linked audit file remains the record of what the broker decided and attempted.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="trace" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.2 · one trace across two processes</span>
        <h2>Follow one run from agent to provider.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="data-journey-map">
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-host">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>telemetry sender 01</span><strong><code>dekopon-run</code></strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>run</small><strong><code>runner.command</code></strong><code>one CLI command</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>model</small><strong><code>prompt.model_turn</code></strong><code>tokens + result</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>tool</small><strong><code>prompt.script</code></strong><code>command spans below</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ W3C traceParent crosses the private socket</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-guest">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>telemetry sender 02</span><strong><code>dekopon-brokerd</code></strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>broker</small><strong><code>broker.invocation</code></strong><code>same trace ID</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>permission</small><strong><code>broker.authorize</code></strong><code>allowed / denied / replay</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>provider</small><strong><code>provider.invoke</code></strong><code>HTTP span below</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>The runner and broker each send their own telemetry. The broker never acts as a relay. An optional <code>traceParent</code> joins the two parts of the trace. Dekopon treats it as untrusted data, checks it strictly, and never uses it for identity, permissions, replay protection, routing, or audit.</p>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>W3C</span><h3><code>traceParent</code></h3><p>Joins spans in the telemetry backend. A malformed value rejects the request instead of creating a false parent relationship.</p></article>
        <article><span>Dekopon</span><h3><code>TraceId</code></h3><p>Ties sessions and requests together in the protocol and durable audit. It has a different job and lifetime from the OpenTelemetry trace ID.</p></article>
        <article><span>independent</span><h3>Two senders</h3><p>Runner telemetry shows model and local work. Broker telemetry shows permission checks and networked provider work. You need both for the full picture.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="logs" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.3 · logs with a purpose</span>
        <h2>Keep logs for facts that need to last.</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Earlier builds logged a start and finish for work that now has a real span. Version 0.2.0 removes that duplication. A span already has start time, parent, duration, and result. Copying the same facts into logs adds noise and storage cost.</p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Log event</th><th scope="col">Why keep it</th><th scope="col">What it records</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>accounting.model.turn</code></th><td>Model calls still cost money or quota after traces expire.</td><td>turn, duration, counts, normalized token usage, result</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>accounting.http.request</code></th><td>Outside calls consume rate limits and operating budget.</td><td>method, host, status, request/response bytes, result</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">refusal / error events</th><td>Important failures need their own retention and search.</td><td>stable categories and IDs for investigation—never raw untrusted errors</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>agent.model.*</code></th><td>Conversation text can be large and does not belong in span fields.</td><td>full prompt/answer only after explicit payload opt-in</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>agent.tool.*</code></th><td>Scripts and output can also be large.</td><td>full script/output only after explicit payload opt-in</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Token usage intentionally appears in two places. The <code>prompt.model_turn</code> span explains one call. The accounting log survives trace sampling and adds up work over time. If a model does not report a token count, the field stays absent instead of pretending the value was zero.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="payloads" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.4 · private by default</span>
        <h2>Keep useful context. Leave private data out.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Included by default</th><th scope="col">Left out by default</th><th scope="col">Never added by payload mode</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>service, model, provider, capability IDs</td><td>prompts and model answers</td><td>bearer tokens and provider credentials</td></tr>
            <tr><td>timings, counts, outcomes, source locations</td><td>model-authored scripts and their output</td><td>OTLP authorization headers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>command kind, argument count, exit code</td><td>provider input and output</td><td>HTTP request/response headers or bodies</td></tr>
            <tr><td>HTTP method, host, status, and byte counts</td><td>URL path and query</td><td>payloads in durable audit</td></tr>
            <tr><td>request, capability, and trace links</td><td>agent request input</td><td>raw untrusted error text</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Use <code>--otel-telemetry-payloads true</code>, <code>DEKOPON_OTEL_TELEMETRY_PAYLOADS</code>, or broker <code>telemetryPayloads: true</code> to include selected request, URL, and transcript data. This adds content, never credentials. The durable audit record does not change.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>Redaction is built into the data type.</strong>
        <p><code>Redacted&lt;T&gt;</code> prints and serializes as a marker. Code must make an explicit call to reveal the value. Persisting one of the few intended secrets also requires an explicit serializer on that field.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="openobserve" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.5 · your telemetry backend</span>
        <h2>Open standards, proven with OpenObserve.</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Dekopon sends standard OpenTelemetry data over HTTP or gRPC. The repository includes a one-container OpenObserve example and an end-to-end CI test. The test starts OpenObserve, runs a real provider, searches traces and logs, checks the required spans and correlation, and confirms that a sentinel input stayed out of telemetry.</p>

      <pre><code><span class="code-purple">export</span> OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:5080/api/default
<span class="code-purple">export</span> OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS='Authorization=Basic%20&lt;TOKEN&gt;,organization=default,stream-name=dekopon'
<span class="code-purple">export</span> OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=dekopon-run

dekopon-run prompt --model qwen3 'Upcase hello' \
  --provider echo-provider.wasm</code></pre>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>HTTP</span><h3>Dekopon adds signal paths</h3><p>The shared endpoint receives <code>/v1/traces</code> and <code>/v1/logs</code>. OpenObserve reads the organization from <code>/api/&lt;org&gt;</code>.</p></article>
        <article><span>gRPC</span><h3>Use the backend address</h3><p>The endpoint contains the host and port. Send <code>organization=&lt;org&gt;</code> through the standard OpenTelemetry headers.</p></article>
        <article><span>both</span><h3>One set of headers</h3><p><code>stream-name=dekopon</code> routes traces and logs. Keep backend credentials in environment headers, never CLI arguments or broker YAML.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p>Use plain HTTP only on loopback or an isolated trusted network. HTTPS uses standard WebPKI roots. Redirects stay off so backend credentials cannot be forwarded elsewhere. A runner command fails if it cannot flush its telemetry. Broker telemetry failures are logged, but do not stop permission checks or replace durable audit.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="footprint" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.6 · measured on the project Raspberry Pi</span>
        <h2>Useful telemetry can stay tiny.</h2>
      </div>

      <article class="footprint-proof">
        <div class="proof-topline"><span></span>OpenObserve · physical allocated blocks</div>
        <div class="proof-value">200 KiB</div>
        <h3>Traces + logs in the current Dekopon sample</h3>
        <p>The <code>dekopon</code> streams for the current prompt sample use 148 KiB for traces and 52 KiB for logs on the Raspberry Pi’s NVMe-backed OpenObserve volume.</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>traces</dt><dd>148 KiB</dd></div>
          <div><dt>logs</dt><dd>52 KiB</dd></div>
          <div><dt>combined</dt><dd>200 KiB</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <small>Snapshot: 16 Aug 2026. Physical allocation, not sparse apparent size. The complete OpenObserve stream tree was 1.22 MiB: 388 KiB of all log/trace payload across streams and 860 KiB of indexes, metadata, bloom data, and directory overhead. Values change as ingestion continues.</small>
      </article>

      <p>Not every run will use 200 KiB. Retention, optional payloads, model turns, command count, compaction, and indexing all change the result. This sample simply shows that useful cross-process traces and accounting logs fit comfortably on small self-hosted hardware. The number uses real allocated disk blocks, not a sparse-file estimate.</p>

